# ABORT(3)

## NAME

abort, raise - terminate abnormally, or send a signal to oneself

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

void abort(void);

#include <signal.h>

int raise(int sig);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Abort* sends SIGABRT to the calling process. This causes a signal that normally terminates the
process with a core dump, which may be used for debugging.

**Note:** v7's page said "executes the PDP11 IOT instruction". There is no such instruction
here and none is needed - *abort* is **kill(getpid(), SIGABRT)** and always was on machines
other than the PDP-11. Not even the NAME of that instruction is left: signal 6 is SIGABRT here
and v7's SIGIOT is not defined (signal(2)). SIGABRT is left at SIG_DFL, so the kernel dumps
core for it.

**Note:** *raise* is C11 and had no v7 ancestor. It sends *sig* to the calling process and does
not return until the signal has been handled. **There is nothing to arrange for that here:** a
kill(2) to one's own process raises the signal in the kernel's pending set, and the system
call's own return path delivers it - the handler has run and returned through the signal frame
by the time *raise* yields a value. b6sim delivers at the same point.

*Raise*'s value is *kill*'s, 0 or -1, rather than C11's "nonzero if unsuccessful" - the same
thing said two ways.

## SEE ALSO

signal(2), exit(3), kill(2), assert(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Usually `IOT trap - core dumped` from the shell.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/abort.c, raise.c

## BUGS

**There is no test for abort, deliberately.** Under b6sim a kill(2) the guest does not catch is
serviced by the simulator killing *its own* process - the guest pid is the host pid - so a test
would take the simulator down with the program and report as a harness crash rather than a
result. Under the real kernel it is an ordinary signal and dumps core.

## BESM-6 NOTES

*Raise* is the *only* routine libc gained when signal delivery landed, and the absences around
it are the point.

The kernel builds the signal frame on the user stack and **plants the return path in it:** a
**$77 SYS_sigret** word, copied out above the frame and named as the handler's return address,
so a handler's ordinary return trips an extracode and the kernel reloads the frame.

So there is no trampoline of the kind other ports carry, no libc table shadowing the kernel's
dispositions, and signal(2) still answers with the *true* previous disposition - including the
SIG_DFL a caught signal is reset to on delivery.

It could not have been libc's work in any case: which *half* of a word to resume at lives in
the saved program status, and only the privileged return instruction restores it, so a handler
interrupted from a right-half instruction can be resumed only by the kernel.
